<WebLayout title="UltimateShades — Overclocking Shrine" description="UltimateShades' overclocking shrine: 486 benchmarks, case mods and cooling experiments.">
  <div style="text-align:center">
    <h1 style="font-size:34px;color:#0000ff;text-shadow:2px 2px 0 #00ffff">🕶️ UltimateShades 🕶️</h1>
    <p style="font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:13px">~ the overclocking shrine ~</p>
    <p style="font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:12px">established 1997 · still running too hot</p>
    <marquee scrollamount="5" style="background:#000;color:#00ff00;padding:3px;border:2px inset #808080;font-family:'Courier New',monospace">
      ★ 486 BENCHMARKS ★ CASE MODS ★ THERMAL PASTE ★ SIGN MY GUESTBOOK ★ BEST VIEWED IN 800x600 ★
    </marquee>
  </div>

  <div class="web-columns">
    <div>
      <h2>Welcome to the Shrine</h2>
      <p>
        I'm <b>ultimateshades</b>. I take old computers apart, put them back together with
        better cooling, and then run benchmarks until something smells like ozone. This is
        where I write down what happened.
      </p>
      <p>
        My friend <a href="/web/">pants</a> says my 486 is "objectively too fast for its own
        good." I am choosing to interpret that as a compliment.
      </p>

      <h2>Bench Log</h2>
      <div class="web-panel">
        <h3>Entry #12: AMD DX4-120 at 133 MHz</h3>
        <div class="web-post-date">Nov 01, 1998 // 02:17 AM</div>
        <p>
          Swapped the stock heatsink for a Pentium-class cooler and added a tiny 40mm fan.
          The DX4-120 is now running at 133 MHz with the bus at 33 MHz. Quake timedemo went
          from 14.8 to 16.1 fps. The case is still open. Do not tell my landlord.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="web-panel">
        <h3>Entry #11: the great thermal paste experiment</h3>
        <div class="web-post-date">Oct 24, 1998 // 08:42 PM</div>
        <p>
          Tested three different thermal compounds on the same heatsink. The difference was
          about 2°C, which is within the margin of error of my thermometer and also my
          patience. The real win was lapping the heatsink. It is now flat enough to use as a
          mirror for checking the POST screen.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="web-panel">
        <h3>Entry #10: Pentium 166 goes to 200</h3>
        <div class="web-post-date">Oct 19, 1998 // 01:03 AM</div>
        <p>
          Set the multiplier to 3x and the bus to 66 MHz. It boots, it runs, and it finishes
          a full round of Solitaire. I am calling that stable. Kylie says I should run a
          proper burn-in test. I say Solitaire <i>is</i> the burn-in test.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Cool Tech Links</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/web/">Pants Place 98</a> — the best 486 site on the web</li>
        <li><a href="/web/~kylie/">Kylie's Cyber Sanctuary</a> — cats, C++ and EFnet</li>
        <li><a href="/web/downloads/">MIDI Archive</a> — free music for your Winamp</li>
        <li><a href="/web/webring/">The Old Net Webring</a> — a ring of retro sites</li>
        <li><a href="/web/search/">AltaVista / The Old Net Search</a> — search the old web</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <aside>
      <div class="web-panel" style="text-align:center">
        <img src="/icons/computer.svg" width="64" height="64" alt="My computer" />
        <p style="font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:12px">
          AMD DX4-120 @ 133<br />32 MB RAM<br />Windows 98
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="web-panel">
        <h3>The Rig</h3>
        <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:12px">
